WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.840
If you stayed through the end-credits of 2016's
Deadpool, you probably heard the lead character

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.950
say this:

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.970
"The sequel, we're gonna have Cable."

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.440
No, he wasn't talking about upgrading his
TV package.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.390
Cable is actually a longtime member of the
X-Men family of Marvel Comics, not to mention

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:18.240
a constant foil for Deadpool.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.730
But with all that said, why hasn't Cable gotten
his own solo film?

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.800
Here's a look at why Hollywood won't give
Cable a movie.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.579
Name recognition

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:30.509
Never say never, but it's kind of hard to
imagine many people going out to see a movie

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:32.560
about the Cable guy.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.110
And there's some historical precedent to back
this up:

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.080
"Well maybe I shouldn't have come at all.,
JERK-OFF!"

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.030
Outside of comics fans, few people have even
heard of him — and if they have, they probably

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:47.100
don't know much more than his name, which
isn't really much to go on.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.100
Even if you didn't know who Iron Man and the
Hulk were, you could probably guess who they

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.920
are and what they do.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:53.920
Cable?

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.670
That lack of name recognition is a huge hurdle,
especially given the multitude of other issues

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.560
facing the character.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.990
Simple name, complicated origins

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.269
Cable's comics backstory sounds like an outtake
from Drunk History.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.360
"I wanna be called Snorkie."

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.070
Ready to get confused?

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:13.750
Strap in: Cable is the son of Cyclops and
a woman named Madelyne Pryor, who is actually

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.430
a demonic clone of Jean Grey.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.970
He suffers from a techno-organic virus, which
slowly replaced his body parts with liquid

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.780
metal and organic steel, threatening to kill
him.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.310
His only hope is a time-traveling being made
of pure energy named Sister Askani, who appeared

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:31.140
to Cyclops in a vision and took young Nathan
2,000 years into the future, believing him

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.190
to be the savior destined to deliver them
from Apocalypse.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.010
"Because Apocalypse is behind the techno virus."

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.370
"Apocalypse, yeah right.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.220
Teamed up with the tooth fairy."

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.060
Lots of time-traveling shenanigans ensued,
and eventually he landed in the present day

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.780
Marvel universe, older than his parents.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.360
And that's just the backstory that led up
to his first appearance in 1990!

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:03.420
Meanwhile, Peter Parker was bitten by a spider.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.340
Fashion don't

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.729
Cable is something of a joke in certain circles,
held up as an example of comic books' convoluted

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:13.230
plots and the excesses of character design
in the 1990s.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.180
Famed comic artist Alex Ross broke down everything
that's unappealing about the character when

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.319
he once said Cable "looked like they just
threw up everything on the character—the

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:26.090
scars, the thing going on with his eye, the
arm, and what's with all the guns?"

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.720
Powerfully problematic

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.500
Let's talk about Cable's powers.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:35.010
Like his mother, he's telekinetic, able to
manipulate matter with his mind, and his cybernetic

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.260
eye and arm give him enhanced vision and super
strength.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:39.260
Also?

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:44.420
He can travel through time, he's built like
a redwood tree and loves gigantic Nerf-style

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.450
guns.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.819
In other words, he's way too powerful, and
seems like he was designed by a committee

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:51.590
of ten-year-old boys.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:52.989
Which isn't far from wrong.

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.300
"You created X-Force?"

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.300
"Mmhmm"

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.300
"So what is this drawing of?"

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.300
"This the Spikeman."

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.300
"And what's this right here?"

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.180
"This is the camera on top of your head that
will record the wrongdoings of others."

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.640
"So Rob, have you had any formal art training?"

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.640
"No."

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:06.879
Reruns

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:11.310
One big question for adapting Cable to the
big screen: what the heck do you do with him?

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.599
His big thing is time traveling to change
the past, but X-Men covered that already with

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:16.940
Days of Future Past.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.140
And his archenemy, Apocalypse?

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.310
Yeah, they did that one already too in 2016,
with X-Men: Apocalypse.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:28.129
If the filmmakers wanted to use Cable, they
had two golden opportunities to include him,

00:03:28.129 --> 00:03:32.840
but they chose not to, for reasons that are
probably becoming clear.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:33.840
Super sidekick

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.970
It's perfect that Cable is set to appear in
the forthcoming Deadpool sequel, because Deadpool's

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:43.440
satirical poke-fun-at-the-genre style is a
match made in heaven for Cable's convoluted

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.510
and over-serious backstory.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:45.510
"It's a big house.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.050
It's funny that I only ever see two of you.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.300
It's almost like the studio couldn't afford
another X Man."

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.770
In fact, there's a long, rich history in the
comics exploring the comedy gold to be mined

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.110
from the pairing.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.200
But now that he's set to debut as Deadpool's
sidekick, it seems pretty unlikely that he'll

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.990
actually graduate to his own movie.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:09.250
But hey, stranger things have happened.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.909
Major risk, small reward

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:15.620
Here's the bottom line: when a character needs
so much reimagining to be viable on-screen,

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.190
it's a major problem.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.299
Look no further than fan-favorite Galactus
for proof.

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:24.240
In the comics, he's a purple-suited giant
who eats planets for breakfast, lunch, and

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.240
dinner.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.430
But the filmmakers behind 2007's Fantastic
Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer opted to portray

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.540
Galactus as a giant fart cloud in space.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.490
Comic fans hated it, and films fans hated
it too, because...really.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:39.720
A cloud?

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:46.800
Even Parallax thinks that's stupid.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.280
At least that was a fart cloud with a face.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.590
Fox faces the same issue with Cable: if they
remain true to what made the character popular

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:58.460
in the comics, film fans will likely just
be confused, and if they don't, comic fans

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.560
will be disappointed.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:04.720
It's a lose-lose scenario, which means that
for now, the big screen will continue to suffer

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.080
from an ongoing Cable outage.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.210
Thanks for watching!

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.660
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.490
And leave us a comment to let us know if you
think Cable should get his own movie...

